{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789469568,"nonce":"9cwiEqFJbln0iTbg2OW1Y+FSaUo=","title":"The collector scans stacks it cannot place, and a host's threads never reach its stop","message":"Two gaps under a host (caribou, issue c646b43 there):\n\n1. `conservative_stack_ranges` walks the running chain from the current krio fiber through its own fibers by `caller_sp`, and takes whatever the chain ends on as the thread's own stack up to its top. A thread inside a fiber the host made, or whose chain resumes from one, is scanned as (a probe on that fiber's stack, the thread's top): a range spanning whatever lies between two mappings. Fix: scan only what can be placed, a fiber of wren_lift's or the thread's own stack (its bounds are now recorded, `thread_stack_bounds`, `ThreadState::stack_lo`); a thread whose chain cannot be placed is left to the host's collector, which sees every stack.\n\n2. `World::stop` waits for every thread to be safe, and a thread running the host's own code polls neither the page nor the interpreter loop. A `host_poll(on)` slot tells the host when a stop is asked for and let go, so a host whose threads reach safepoints of its own brings them to wren_lift's there. With it: `leave_safe_here` waits through the seam's `thread_safe`/`thread_running`, since the host's collector may need the thread meanwhile; a view a host's world made on a worker goes with the program's last task on that thread, so a program is held by nothing once its main view and its tasks are gone; and the main view's drop leaves safe again after joining the pool, balancing its `enter_safe` for the seam.","files":null}]}